<Legal
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="What PetPomo stores, what it does not, and why the weather feature needs no location permission."
  updated="2026-08-16"
  intro="The short version: your save file lives in your browser, there is no account, and we never learn who you are."
>
  <h2>What is stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    Your progress — focus sessions, coins, what you have bought, your pet's mood, and your settings — is written to
    your browser's <strong>local storage</strong>. It never leaves your device unless you switch on cloud sync. Clearing
    your browser data deletes it permanently; there is no copy for us to restore.
  </p>

  <h2>Cloud sync, if you turn it on</h2>
  <p>
    Cloud sync is off by default. When you enable it, your browser generates a random 24-character code and uploads
    your save file under that code. The code is the only credential — we do not ask for an email address, and there
    is no account to log in to.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>We store an opaque blob of game state. We cannot tell who it belongs to.</li>
    <li>Anyone holding your sync code can read and overwrite that save, so treat it like a password.</li>
    <li>We never email it, never log it, and it only travels over an encrypted connection.</li>
    <li>Lose the code and the save is unreachable — for you and for us.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Weather and your location</h2>
  <p>
    The world in the game follows the real weather where you are. Getting that <strong>does not use your device's
    location</strong> and never triggers a permission prompt.
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead, our host (Cloudflare) attaches an approximate location to every web request as it arrives, the same way
    any website can see roughly where a visitor is connecting from. Before we use it for anything, we round it to one
    decimal place — an area of roughly 11&nbsp;km — and that rounded value is all that is ever sent to the weather
    provider, <a href="https://open-meteo.com" rel="noopener noreferrer nofollow" target="_blank">Open-Meteo</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rounded coordinates are used as a cache key so that everyone in the same area shares a single lookup. Nothing
    is written to our database, nothing is logged, and no record is created against you. If you would rather not have
    this at all, blocking <code>/api/weather</code> is harmless — the game falls back to fair weather.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No accounts, no passwords, no email addresses.</li>
    <li>No advertising or analytics trackers, and no third-party cookies.</li>
    <li>No selling or sharing of personal information, because we do not collect any.</li>
    <li>No profiling and no automated decision-making about you.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If advertising is introduced later, this page will be updated <em>before</em> it goes live, and the change will be
    described plainly rather than buried.
  </p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>
    PetPomo sets no cookies. Local storage is used instead, and it is used only for your own game data. See the
    <a href="/cookies">cookie notice</a> for detail.
  </p>

  <h2>Server logs</h2>
  <p>
    Our host keeps short-lived operational logs (things like request counts and error rates) to keep the service
    running and to defend against abuse. We do not build user profiles from them.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    PetPomo is suitable for all ages and collects no personal information from anyone, children included.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Because we hold no personal data, there is normally nothing to request, correct or erase. If you use cloud sync
    and want that save deleted, send us the sync code from
    <a href="/contact">the contact page</a> and we will delete the row. Since we cannot verify who owns a code, please
    do not send one that is not yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    Material changes will be reflected in the “last updated” date above. Continuing to use the site after a change
    means you accept it.
  </p>
</Legal>
